          "type": "turn",
          "question": "My blood sugar has been over 20 all day and I feel sick. How urgent is that, and what should I be told about diabetic emergencies?",
          "answer": "**Please contact a doctor or urgent care now, today. Call emergency services if you have any of the warning signs below.** A blood sugar over 20 that lasts all day, with you feeling sick, can be dangerous. The automated check found no matching emergency pattern, but that does not mean you are safe. It only means the check did not recognise your description.\n\n**Call emergency services straight away if you have:**\n- Vomiting, or you cannot keep fluids down\n- Deep or fast breathing\n- Stomach pain\n- Drowsiness, confusion, or trouble staying awake\n\nThe diabetes guidance in this system says that very high blood sugar with nausea, vomiting, abdominal pain, deep or rapid breathing, drowsiness, or confusion \"may indicate a hyperglycemic emergency and requires immediate medical attention\" (*Type 2 diabetes mellitus*, general clinical reference). Illness also raises blood sugar, and the guidance warns that stopping glucose-lowering medication because someone is not eating is a known route into this kind of emergency (*Sticking with a diabetes regimen*, national diabetes association guidance). It recommends a sick-day plan agreed in advance with your own clinician. This system cannot give you that plan or any dosing advice.\n\n**What to do now:**\n- Contact your doctor, diabetes team, or a helpline today, and tell them your readings and symptoms.\n- Do not wait for your next routine appointment.\n- Keep someone with you if you can.\n\nThe sources I was given do not cover what to do at home for readings this high, and I cannot advise on medication changes.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
